menu "VESC CAN"

    config VESC_CAN_TX_GPIO
        int "TWAI TX GPIO"
        default 20
        help
            GPIO connected to TX of the CAN transceiver
            (e.g. SN65HVD230). Pick a pin not used by the BSP.

    config VESC_CAN_RX_GPIO
        int "TWAI RX GPIO"
        default 24
        help
            GPIO connected to RX of the CAN transceiver.

    config VESC_CAN_SPEED_KBPS
        int "CAN baud rate (kbps)"
        default 500
        help
            VESC controllers default to 500 kbps. Allowed values:
            125, 250, 500, 1000.

    config VESC_CAN_CONTROLLER_ID
        int "Display CAN ID (this device)"
        default 2
        range 1 254
        help
            CAN ID announced by the display on the bus. Must be
            different from any other VESC node. 255 is reserved
            for broadcast.

    config VESC_CAN_TARGET_ID
        int "Target VESC ID"
        default 10
        range 1 254
        help
            CAN ID of the VESC controller polled for RT data.

    config VESC_CAN_RT_INTERVAL_MS
        int "RT data poll interval (ms)"
        default 100
        range 20 1000

    config VESC_CAN_LISP_INTERVAL_MS
        int "LISP stats poll interval (ms)"
        default 100
        range 20 1000

    config VESC_CAN_LISP_POLL_ENABLE
        bool "Enable LISP stats poll"
        default n
        help
            Polls COMM_LISP_GET_STATS at the configured rate. Only
            enable if your VESC has a LISP application loaded.

    config VESC_CAN_POLL_START_DELAY_MS
        int "Hold off all CAN polling for this long after boot (ms)"
        default 4000
        range 0 30000
        help
            The display goes quiet on CAN for this long after start-up before
            it begins polling (RT data, IO, the dashboard DASH packet and the
            quick-action panel). Nothing is dropped — the pollers just start
            later.

            Why: a second node on the bus (the ESP32-C3 BLE helper) has to get
            its own request/reply exchange with the VESC's LISP script through,
            and it loses that race whenever the display is already streaming
            requests. Observed on hardware: with the boot splash enabled — which
            delays the display's CAN bring-up by several seconds — pedal assist
            over the helper works; without the splash the helper reports
            "no data from vesc" and PAS never commands current. This makes the
            quiet window explicit instead of a side effect of how long the boot
            animation happens to run.

            The root cause of the dead pedal assist was a node dropping into
            TWAI bus-off and never being recovered — fixed properly by
            can_health_check() in comm_can.c. This hold-off is kept on top of
            that as cheap insurance: it costs a few seconds of empty dashboard
            at boot and keeps us off the bus while the other nodes and the
            VESC's LISP script come up, which is when the error storms that
            lead to bus-off are worst.

            0 disables it.

endmenu
